Neutrality is not merely a slogan. It shapes information barriers, customer support, intellectual-property protection, capacity allocation and capital spending. Trust can take years to build and can be lost quickly. A neutral foundry also needs design infrastructure: process design kits, verified reusable IP and relationships with electronic-design-automation vendors so customers can produce layouts the factory can build.

Consider an illustrative startup with a promising accelerator. Under an IDM-only world, it must either raise billions for a factory, accept a competitor as manufacturer or keep the design on an older accessible process. Under a pure-play model, it can buy wafer capacity and focus scarce capital on architecture, software and customer adoption. The foundry does not make the startup risk-free, but it changes which risks the startup must own.

Pure-play is a model, not a guarantee of leadership

Manufacturing neutrality does not automatically produce the best technology, lowest cost or enough capacity. Customers still compare process performance, yield, packaging, design support, delivery reliability, geographic exposure and price. A foundry with weak execution remains a weak supplier.

Nor are the categories perfectly clean. Samsung operates a foundry business while the wider company also sells memory, logic and finished electronics. Its foundry materials emphasize process technology, design ecosystems and integrated packaging, but potential customers must judge how the larger corporate structure affects trust and execution.

Intel is attempting another hybrid. Its product businesses remain major internal users of Intel Foundry, while Intel also seeks external customers. Intel’s 2025 annual report says external foundry and assembly/test revenue was $307 million and that nearly all foundry activity still supported internal manufacturing. The filing also says Intel may pause or discontinue pursuit of its next leading-edge process after 18A if it cannot secure a significant external customer. Internal scale can help fund a process; external demand is still needed to prove that the service is competitive beyond the parent company.

The reusable analytical test

When evaluating any foundry, separate four questions.

First, can it manufacture the required chip with competitive performance and yield? Second, can customers design for the process using dependable tools, IP and support? Third, will customers trust it with sensitive designs and long product roadmaps? Fourth, can enough profitable volume support the enormous fixed investment?

A company can pass one test and fail another. A technically strong process without external trust may remain mostly captive. A neutral manufacturer without leading execution may win specialty work but not the most demanding designs. A large customer ecosystem can strengthen manufacturing learning, yet concentration in a few customers can also create bargaining and capacity risks.

Transfer question

Suppose a new foundry announces a leading-edge process and offers prices below TSMC. Does that establish a credible alternative?

Answer: no. Price and a process announcement address only part of the decision. A credible alternative also needs measured yield, reliable volume, design tools and IP, packaging access, schedule performance, protection of customer information and evidence that customers will commit important products. The business model matters because trust and shared scale help convert technical capability into an ecosystem.

The structural lesson

TSMC’s foundry model lowered the capital threshold for chip design and helped make the fabless semiconductor company practical at scale. It reorganized who could participate: designers could specialize without owning factories, while the foundry specialized in process technology and manufacturing.

